WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.680
Why records matter: cash in your bag is not profit.

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:07.400
This is the video for Module 1, Why records matter.

3
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:17.590
A business record is anything written down that shows what happened in your business. A sale, a purchase, money paid out, goods given on credit.

4
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.570
The important thing is that it is written at the time it happens, so you do not need to remember it later.

5
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:36.340
Keeping everything in your head works with one product and a few customers. With ten products, credit customers and transfers, the head forgets and remembers the good days more than the bad ones.

6
00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:48.830
Your records help you see if you are really making money. They help your family run the shop when you are sick, and they help your cooperative or microfinance bank when you ask for a loan.

7
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:56.180
This is the most important idea in the whole course. Money in your hand is not the same as profit.

8
00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.080
For example, Bisi sells fresh pepper and tomatoes in a Lagos market.

9
00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:10.110
By evening she counts about 38,000 naira in her bag. Without a record she thinks, I made 38,000 naira today.

10
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:25.150
But that morning she bought tomatoes and pepper for about 30,000 naira. And she paid about 2,000 naira for transport and 500 naira for the market ticket. That is 2,500 naira of costs.

11
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.860
So her real profit is about 5,500 naira, not 38,000.

12
00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:41.340
If she spends 10,000 naira on household things tonight, she eats into the money she needs for tomorrow's stock. That is called eating the capital.

13
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:50.050
There are five records that answer almost every question a small trader has. The sales record shows how much you sold.

14
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.380
The expenses record shows where your money is going.

15
00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.930
The stock record shows what you have left, and what is missing.

16
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.170
The debtors list shows who took goods on credit, how much, and when they promised to pay.

17
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.500
And the cash book shows how much money you should have right now.

18
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:21.280
If five feels like too much, start with sales and expenses. Add the debtors list the very first time someone buys on credit, because credit is where traders lose the most money.

19
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:35.780
Most money leaks out quietly. For example, Amina sells drinks and snacks at a motor park. She loses about 500 naira a day to snacks on credit that are never paid, and things taken by children.

20
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.530
She works 6 days a week. That is 3,000 naira a week.

21
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:47.050
There are about 52 weeks in a year, so that is about 156,000 naira.

22
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:56.870
One crate of soft drinks costs her about 6,000 naira. So a leak she thought was nothing costs her about 26 crates of stock a year.

23
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:10.670
The best method is the one you will use every day. A notebook is cheap and needs no data. Use a pen, not a pencil. If you make a mistake, draw one line through it. Never tear out pages.

24
00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:21.030
A phone adds up for you and can save a copy online. Many traders write in a notebook during the day and type the totals into the phone at night.

25
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:32.240
For example, Kemi sells bread and cannot write easily. She draws a small loaf and a big loaf, and makes one tally mark under the right picture for each sale.

26
00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:35.390
Credit sales are marked in red.

27
00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:52.620
At night she counts 23 small loaves at about 700 naira, and 11 big loaves at about 1,500 naira. Together, about 32,600 naira of bread, with 3,600 naira owed to her.

28
00:03:53.870 --> 00:04:07.410
Some people show a fake transfer screen or a fake SMS alert and walk away with goods. Before you release goods, check that the money has entered your own account. Record the transfer only after you confirm it.

29
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:16.170
If your records are on a phone, set a screen lock, never share your bank PIN or one-time codes with anyone, and back up regularly.

30
00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:33.480
Now get an exercise book, a biro and a ruler. Set up your first page with three columns: what happened, money in, and money out. A page of honest, untidy numbers is better than a beautiful empty book. Continue with the lessons below.
